GitHub is both a backup tool and a professional portfolio. What you put there is what clients and employers see when they search your name.

Why the Portfolio Matters.

A GitHub profile with active, well-documented projects communicates competence faster than any CV. When a client asks "can you show me something you've built," you send a link instead of writing a paragraph. The code is the proof.

The goal of this lesson is not to have a perfect profile. It is to have a complete one: pinned projects, a profile README, clean repo descriptions, and consistent commit history.

The GitHub Profile README

A profile README is a special repository with the same name as your GitHub username. GitHub displays its README.md file at the top of your profile page. It is the first thing anyone sees when they visit your profile.

Tip: Create the repository: go to GitHub, click New, name it exactly your username (e.g., brianotieno). GitHub will tell you this is a special repository. Initialize it with a README. Edit the README to introduce yourself and what you build.

For example:

Brian Otieno | Python Developer | Automation & AI Tools

Building practical tools that solve actual problems. Based in Nairobi, Kenya.

What I Build
