Note: The example above uses an em dash for visual separation in the title. In your actual README file that is fine. The no-em-dash rule applies to lesson content, not to profile text you write independently.

Writing a Good README for Each Project

Every repository needs a README.md that answers four questions: What does it do? Why does it exist? How do you run it? What does the output look like?

# File: README.md

# SMP Tracker

A daily performance tracker for the Self-Made Protocol (SMP) fitness program.
Logs sleep, water intake, and step count. Predicts goal achievement using a
trained Random Forest classifier and generates a coaching message.

## What It Does

- Accepts daily check-in data (sleep hours, water glasses, steps)
- Predicts whether the 10,000-step goal will be hit
- Returns a confidence score and a direct coaching message
- Exports a weekly summary report as JSON

## Setup

```bash
pip install -r requirements.txt
```

## Usage

```python
from tracker import analyze_day

result = analyze_day(sleep_hr=7.5, water_glasses=9, bench_kg=88)
print(result["coaching"])
```

## Sample Output

```
Prediction: HIT GOAL (88% confidence)
Coach: Strong inputs, strong output. Baseline is locked in. Keep this pattern consistent.
```

## Stack

Python, scikit-learn, pandas, FastAPI

Which Projects to Pin.

GitHub lets you pin up to six repositories at the top of your profile. Choose projects that show range: one data project, one API project, one web or JavaScript project, and one that demonstrates ML or AI. Four pinned projects with strong READMEs beat twenty repos with no documentation.

smp-tracker
ML classifier + coaching layer. Predicts daily goal achievement from biometric inputs.

Python. scikit-learn. pandas

api-dashboard
Multi-endpoint dashboard pulling live data, grouping by protocol, exporting JSON reports.

Python. FastAPI.  pandas

browser-coach
Client-side JavaScript tool. Prediction engine + coaching generator running entirely in the browser.

JavaScript. DOM. Fetch API

data-analysis-report
28-day SMP fitness log analysis using pandas and NumPy. Weekly breakdown with protocol comparison.

Python. pandas. NumPy

Commit Consistency

The GitHub contribution graph (the green squares on your profile) shows daily commit activity. Clients and recruiters look at it. A consistent pattern of small daily commits is more compelling than three huge commits once a month.

Habit	                                                   What it produces on GitHub
One commit per working session	                  Consistent green squares on the activity graph
Descriptive commit messages	                      Readable history that shows your thinking
Feature branches + PRs	                          A network graph showing disciplined development workflow
Updated READMEs	                                  Projects that look maintained and professional
Public repos (not all private)	                  Work that is visible to anyone who searches for you

Tip: Commit early and often. A commit message that says "Work in progress: add API endpoint skeleton" is fine. It is better than not committing. The history shows you were working. You can always clean up messages later with git rebase -i before a final push.

